<p>Cumin is India's second-largest spice export by value after dried red chilli, and the destination pattern echoes chilli closely: China leads by a clear margin. Calendar 2024 figures show China importing USD 140.7 million of Indian cumin, more than 50% ahead of the second-place market, Bangladesh (USD 91.7 million), out of USD 784.9 million in total Indian cumin exports worldwide across roughly 237,241 MT. The driver is China's scale of food-processing and culinary demand — cumin is a staple in specific regional cuisines and in broader food manufacturing — combined with India's established cleaning, sorting and export-documentation capacity. This guide is for Chinese importers and food manufacturers needing consistent, specification-driven volume.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<p>Bangladesh, the UAE and the United States round out India's top cumin markets in the data above, each at meaningfully smaller scale than China but still significant volumes in their own right. If China isn't your specific market, see our broader <a href="/products/spices/">spices category page</a> for the full picture across destinations.</p>

<h2>Specification checklist</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Form</strong> — whole seed or ground; whole retains aroma longer and is the more common export form</li>
  <li><strong>Purity</strong> — freedom from admixture, other seeds and foreign matter</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture</strong> — affects storage stability and shelf life</li>
  <li><strong>Volatile oil content</strong> — the aroma-intensity parameter that matters most to food manufacturers</li>
</ul>
<p>Specify these clearly and we'll quote against them rather than a generic "export quality" tier.</p>

<h2>Sourcing regions and harvest timing</h2>
<p>Cumin is grown predominantly in Gujarat and Rajasthan, with Unjha — Asia's largest cumin trading market — functioning as the central price-discovery and processing hub. Harvest runs February through April. Well-dried cumin stores for many months without significant quality loss, which supports year-round supply from stock rather than a narrow seasonal window.</p>

<h2>Quality checks worth requesting</h2>
<p>Beyond purity and volatile oil content, ask about admixture screening for other similar-looking seeds, which can be an adulteration risk on cheaper offers in this category generally. A reputable supplier should be able to describe their cleaning and sorting process specifically, not just assert "premium quality" without detail. We can walk you through our sourcing and processing steps for cumin specifically if that level of detail is useful for your own supplier qualification process.</p>

