<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>Cumin is one of the more genuinely volatile spice commodities we handle, and pricing moves more than most year to year. Four things matter most: the size and quality of that year's Gujarat/Rajasthan crop (cumin is a winter-sown, largely rain-fed crop in parts of its growing region, so weather during the growing season has an outsized effect on both volume and quality), Unjha market auction dynamics (as Asia's largest cumin trading hub, Unjha's daily price discovery sets the reference point the whole trade prices against), the cleaning grade you specify (sortex-clean commands a premium over machine-clean for the additional optical-sorting step), and prevailing INR/USD/RMB exchange rates plus freight rates to Chinese ports. Timing within the crop year matters too: pricing typically eases as fresh crop enters the market after the February-April harvest, compared to late-season, older-stock pricing.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific cleaning grade, purity, packaging and destination port — never as a flat market-average rate. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: machine-clean or sortex-clean, singles or splits, target volatile oil range if you have one, quantity, and destination port.</p>
