<h2>What goes wrong on a first order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p>The most common hard stop for a first-time China-bound cumin buyer isn't a quality dispute — it's discovering, mid-negotiation, that GACC facility registration under Decree 248 hasn't been confirmed and that the CIFER registration process has real lead time that can't be compressed once a shipment is already booked. Confirm your supplier's registration status as the very first question, before you get into specification or pricing at all.</p>
<p>On the quality side, the most common dispute is a volatile-oil reading mismatch — like viscosity testing on other products, volatile oil content is sensitive to test method, sample handling and how long the cumin has been stored since testing, so two labs testing the same lot at different times can report meaningfully different figures. Ask what testing method and timing our certificate of analysis reflects, and consider requesting a re-test close to shipment date rather than relying solely on a certificate issued weeks earlier.</p>
<p>China's pesticide maximum residue limits (MRLs) for imported spices are, in our experience, applied more strictly and with more specific testing than some other markets — this isn't a paperwork formality, and a shipment can be held at customs if MRL testing wasn't arranged with the right scope in advance. We recommend confirming which specific residue panel your buyer's customs process expects before the container ships, not after it arrives.</p>
<p>What first-time buyers most often get wrong: treating cumin as a simple, low-differentiation commodity and requesting "export quality" without specifying cleaning grade, purity or volatile oil range — this is the single most common cause of quote confusion and mismatched expectations we see on this product, particularly from buyers new to spice sourcing rather than seafood or grain.</p>
